# Everything. This project is hand-formatted, and Prettier is not its
# formatter — this file is what makes that true rather than merely intended.
#
# Two reasons, one of them load-bearing:
#
# 1. MDX. Prettier's *markdown* parser normalises `*` emphasis to `_`, and an
#    editor that treats `.mdx` as Markdown (VS Code's default, without an MDX
#    extension) will rewrite `{/* … */}` comment blocks to `{/_ … _/}`. That is
#    not a comment to MDX: it parses as a JSX expression holding `/_ … _/`,
#    which oxc reads as an unterminated regular expression. The build dies with
#    a message pointing at the delimiter instead of at the formatter that moved
#    it. This has already happened once, to agent-versioning.mdx.
#
#    Even with the correct `mdx` parser it is unsafe here. Case studies have a
#    hard rule that a closing tag must sit flush left (see CLAUDE.md), because
#    an indented `</Section>` after a numbered list reads to CommonMark as a
#    list continuation and the element never closes. Reindentation is exactly
#    what a formatter does.
#
# 2. Everything else. Prettier has never run on this repo. Measured against the
#    codebase, its config-less defaults rewrite all 42 src TS/TSX files, and
#    even a config tuned to match (tabs, single quotes, printWidth 110) still
#    restructures 20 of them — Prettier's line-breaking decisions are its own
#    and no setting makes them agree with hand-authored code. So there is no
#    .prettierrc: a config would imply Prettier owns this formatting, and it
#    does not.
#
# `.editorconfig` beside this is what actually keeps indentation consistent,
# and it applies to every editor rather than to one tool.
#
# To opt a path back in, delete its line — or narrow the `*` below. Do that
# deliberately, and never for src/content.
*
